+# internal-api-client
+
+Thin, synchronous Python client for the internal reports/exports API (`/v2`).
+Requires Python 3.11+.
+
+## Quick start
+
+```python
+from datetime import datetime, timezone
+
+from src import ApiClient, ApiError, ClientConfig
+
+config = ClientConfig.from_env()           # INTERNAL_API_URL, INTERNAL_API_TOKEN
+client = ApiClient(config, transport)      # transport: see "Transport" below
+
+reports = client.list_reports("globex", since=datetime(2024, 3, 1, tzinfo=timezone.utc))
+exports = client.list_exports("globex", fmt="xlsx")
+```
+
+## Configuration
+
+`ClientConfig` is a frozen dataclass:
+
+| Field                   | Default                            | Meaning                                                      |
+|-------------------------|------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|
+| `base_url`              | `https://api.internal.example.com` | API root; a trailing `/` is stripped                          |
+| `token`                 | `""`                               | Bearer token                                                  |
+| `timeout_seconds`       | `10.0`                             | Passed to the transport on every send                         |
+| `retry_attempts`        | `5`                                | Total attempts for the 400-retry on `list_reports`            |
+| `retry_backoff_seconds` | `0.5`                              | Base for the linear 400-retry backoff                         |
+| `rate_limit_retries`    | `3`                                | Extra sends after an HTTP 429 (`0` disables)                  |
+| `user_agent`            | `internal-api-client/0.4`          | `User-Agent` header value                                     |
+
+`ClientConfig.from_env()` reads only `INTERNAL_API_URL` and `INTERNAL_API_TOKEN`;
+every other field keeps its default. If `INTERNAL_API_TOKEN` is unset the token is
+empty and the server will answer 401 (surfaced as `ApiError`).
+
+## Transport
+
+The client does no HTTP itself. It takes any object implementing
+`src.transport.Transport`:
+
+```python
+def send(self, request: Request, timeout: float) -> Response: ...
+```
+
+In production this is the host service's `platform-http` transport, injected at
+startup. Tests use the in-memory fake in `tests/fake_api.py`. The constructor also
+accepts a `sleep` callable (default `time.sleep`) used for all retry waits, which is
+handy for tests.
+
+## Endpoints
+
+| Method                                            | Request                     | Returns         |
+|---------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------|
+| `list_reports(account, since, until=None)`        | `GET /v2/reports`           | `list[Report]`  |
+| `get_report(report_id)`                           | `GET /v2/reports/{id}`      | `Report`        |
+| `list_exports(account, fmt="csv", created_after=None)` | `GET /v2/exports`      | `list[Export]`  |
+| `get_export(export_id)`                           | `GET /v2/exports/{id}`      | `Export`        |
+| `search_accounts(query)`                          | `GET /v2/accounts/search?q=`| `list[Account]` |
+
+List endpoints read the `items` array of the JSON response. Model timestamp fields
+(`Report.period_start`, `Export.created_at`) are returned as the raw strings the
+server sent; they are not parsed into `datetime`.
+
+## How requests are built
+
+- **Method:** always `GET`. There is no request body.
+- **URL:** `base_url.rstrip("/") + path`. Resource IDs in `get_report` / `get_export`
+  are inserted into the path **as-is, without percent-encoding** — pass only plain
+  IDs as returned by the API (no `/`, `?`, `#`, spaces, etc.).
+- **Query string:** parameters whose value is `None` are omitted (e.g. `until`,
+  `created_after` when not given). The rest are encoded with
+  `urllib.parse.urlencode`, in the order shown in the method signature. Spaces become
+  `+` and reserved characters are percent-encoded, so account names like
+  `Smith & Co` are sent as `account=Smith+%26+Co`.
+- **Headers** (identical on every request; no per-call overrides):
+  - `Accept: application/json`
+  - `Authorization: Bearer <token>`
+  - `User-Agent: <user_agent>`
+- **Timeout:** `timeout_seconds` is passed to the transport on each individual send,
+  including every retry. It is not an overall deadline for the call.
+
+## Dates and times
+
+The two date-filtered endpoints use **different formats**:
+
+| Endpoint       | Parameter(s)     | Sent as                                        | Example for `2024-01-01 00:00 UTC` |
+|----------------|------------------|------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|
+| `list_reports` | `since`, `until` | America/Bogota local time, **no offset**       | `2023-12-31T19:00:00`              |
+| `list_exports` | `created_after`  | UTC with `Z` suffix                            | `2024-01-01T00:00:00Z`             |
+
+Rules that apply to both:
+
+- **Naive datetimes are treated as UTC**, not as the machine's local time. Pass
+  timezone-aware datetimes to avoid surprises.
+- Aware datetimes in any timezone are converted correctly.
+- Seconds are the finest resolution; microseconds are truncated (dropped, not rounded).
+
+Reports-specific notes:
+
+- The reports server has no timezone support, so the client converts to Bogota time
+  (fixed `UTC-05:00`; Colombia does not observe DST) before sending.
+- `since` is inclusive and `until` is exclusive against a report's `period_start`.
+- `Report.period_start` values come back in the same offset-less form; they are not
+  converted back to UTC by the client.
+
+## Errors
+
+- Any final response with **status ≥ 400** (after the retries described below) raises
+  `src.models.ApiError`, with:
+  - `status` — the HTTP status code
+  - `path` — the endpoint path, without base URL or query string (e.g. `/v2/exports/e-1`)
+  - `body` — the raw response body (`bytes`); the exception message includes its
+    first 200 bytes
+- Responses below 400 are treated as success and their body is parsed as JSON.
+- The client does **not** wrap other failures. These propagate unchanged:
+  - exceptions raised by the transport (timeouts, connection errors, ...)
+  - `json.JSONDecodeError` / `UnicodeDecodeError` for an unparsable success body
+  - `KeyError` / `ValueError` if the JSON doesn't match the expected model shape
+
+```python
+try:
+    export = client.get_export(export_id)
+except ApiError as exc:
+    if exc.status == 404:
+        ...
+    raise
+```
+
+## Retries
+
+There are two independent retry mechanisms. Nothing else is retried — **not 5xx
+responses, not other 4xx responses, and not transport exceptions.**
+
+### Rate limiting (HTTP 429) — all endpoints
+
+When a response is `429`, the client sleeps and resends the identical request, up to
+`rate_limit_retries` times (default 3, i.e. at most 4 sends). If the last response is
+still `429`, `ApiError(429, ...)` is raised.
+
+The wait comes from the `Retry-After` header (header name matched case-insensitively):
+
+- a non-negative integer number of seconds is used as-is — **there is no upper cap**,
+  so a large `Retry-After` blocks the calling thread for that long;
+- a missing, empty, non-numeric, negative, or HTTP-date value falls back to **1 second**.
+
+### Transient 400s — `list_reports` only
+
+`GET /v2/reports` (the list endpoint only; `get_report` is not included) is known to
+return spurious `400`s for some users, so the client retries it on `400`:
+
+- up to `retry_attempts` total attempts (default 5);
+- linear backoff of `retry_backoff_seconds * n` before retry *n*: with defaults
+  0.5s, 1.0s, 1.5s, 2.0s (5s total);
+- if every attempt returns `400`, `ApiError(400, ...)` is raised.
+
+Consequences for callers:
+
+- A genuinely invalid `list_reports` request (e.g. a malformed parameter) also costs
+  the full backoff (~5s with defaults) before the `400` surfaces.
+- Each attempt goes through the 429 handling above with its own budget, so the two
+  mechanisms don't consume each other's retries. In the worst case one
+  `list_reports` call makes `retry_attempts × (rate_limit_retries + 1)` sends
+  (20 with defaults).
+
+The set of paths with 400-retry is `FLAKY_ENDPOINTS` in `src/api_client.py`.
+
+## Endpoint-specific behavior summary
+
+| Endpoint          | Date format                 | 429 retry | 400 retry | Notes                                         |
+|-------------------|-----------------------------|-----------|-----------|-----------------------------------------------|
+| `list_reports`    | Bogota local, no offset     | yes       | yes       | `since` required, inclusive; `until` exclusive |
+| `get_report`      | —                           | yes       | no        | ID not URL-encoded                            |
+| `list_exports`    | UTC, `Z` suffix             | yes       | no        | `fmt` defaults to `csv`, not validated client-side |
+| `get_export`      | —                           | yes       | no        | ID not URL-encoded                            |
+| `search_accounts` | —                           | yes       | no        |                                               |
+
+## Development
+
+```sh
+python -m pytest
+```
